NAME
    scrape - choose games, see what pictures they have, plan and run a scrape

SYNOPSIS
    scrape ls   [<choice>...] [--kinds <kind>,...|theme] [--videos]
    scrape plan [<choice>...] [<option>...]
    scrape run  [<choice>...] [<option>...]
    scrape run  --resume
    scrape status
    scrape wait [scrape<n>]

    choices: --system <es-de name>[,...]  --favorites  --collection <name>
             --missing <kind>[,...]|any|metadata  <path>...  (and paths on
             standard input)
    options: --kinds <kind>,...|theme  --videos  --overwrite
             --region <region>  --language <language>  --threads <n>

DESCRIPTION
    The command of the emusen.scrape module, and the backend of the Scraper
    window. It does not scrape by itself: every request goes to EmuSen's
    one scraper, the emusen-scrape component, through the emusen.scrape
    service, so the quota rules, the order of the sources (ScreenScraper,
    then OpenEmu's sources where switched on) and the media store are the
    scraper's and are the same as from a game's menu. Inside EmuSen the
    shell is lent EmuSen's own open scraper, so a process never holds two
    and never counts the account's quota twice; in a shell with no host
    the platform library opens one from the settings file (the media
    store under Media, games.db under Library) on first use.

    Data lines go to standard output and notes to standard error, so each
    form pipes. Nothing is sent to ScreenScraper except by 'scrape run'.

    The Scraper window and EmuSen's own Scrape Games ask the same command
    as data through the platform library (emusen_scraping_command), so the
    choice has one owner: this module (EmuSen_LibraryTools.md section 9).

  Choosing the games
    The games are the library's (AppSettings.RomDirectory) as the scan
    shelves them, with the packages and compressed discs beside it, each
    under its ES-DE system name. With no choice every game is chosen. The
    choices narrow it, and combine:
        --system      only these systems (snes, megadrive, psx, ...)
        --favorites   only the favourites (--favourites is the same)
        --collection  only the members of the named collection, ignoring
                      case
        --missing     only the games with no picture of the kind from any
                      source (the package, the store, an ES-DE folder, a
                      picture placed by hand, OpenEmu's); a list of kinds
                      means a game with none of them (ES-DE's "No game
                      image" is miximage,screenshot,titlescreen,cover);
                      'any' means a game lacking at least one of the kinds
                      asked; 'metadata' a game with no description, edited
                      or scraped (ES-DE's "No metadata")
        <path>        the named games only ("by hand"): a full path, a
                      path in the shell's tree, or one relative to the
                      library. Paths on standard input, one a line, are the
                      same; a line's first tab-separated field is its path,
                      so the output of 'scrape ls' pipes back in.
    A game marked "Exclude from multi-scraper" is left out unless it is
    named by hand; a note says how many were left out.

  The kinds
    With no --kinds a run asks for the scraper's own default, every picture
    a theme can draw: cover, screenshot, marquee, titlescreen, miximage,
    backcover, 3dbox, physicalmedia and fanart, so that changing themes never
    needs a second scrape (decided 2026-11-02 by the tester, who took the
    window's Theme switch away for that reason). --kinds names any of
    ES-DE's eleven:
    cover, screenshot, marquee, titlescreen, miximage, backcover, 3dbox,
    physicalmedia, fanart, manual, video. --kinds theme is the "what my
    theme shows" preset: for each system, the kinds the active big picture
    theme draws there, as EmuSen's theme loader reads it (the gamelist's
    imageType lists, its video elements, and the media types its variant's
    triggers name). Only a shell inside EmuSen can read the theme; anywhere
    else --kinds theme is refused and the kinds must be named. A run over
    several systems asks for the union of their kinds.

    Videos are off by default. A video is asked for only with --videos,
    which adds it; a video named by --kinds or by the theme is otherwise
    left out, with a note.

  The forms
    'ls' prints one line for each chosen game, tab-separated:
        <path>  <system>  <kind>=<source> ...
    where the source is package, hand, esde, screenscraper, openemu or
    other_game, or '-' for none: the gaps a theme would show, before
    anything is asked.

    'plan' prints what a run would ask, one 'key<TAB>value' a line: games,
    not_yet_asked, asked_again, complete (already holding every kind),
    max_requests (the bound: one lookup and one request a kind),
    expected_requests and expected_mb (from the offer rates measured for
    the scraper), requests_left_today (or unknown), minutes, usable and
    why_not (whether ScreenScraper can be asked now, and why not), kinds.
    Its sentence goes to standard error: "38 game(s), about 70 request(s)
    (at most 145), 9 already complete; ...". The numbers are the scraper's
    ScrapePlan.

    'run' first prints the plan's sentence on standard error, then starts
    the run: a wide run, as Scrape Games from the library makes it, queued
    by priority (one system: console; otherwise library). One line goes to
    standard output as each game finishes:
        <outcome>  <path>  <kinds saved, or ->  [openemu]  [<detail>]
    with the outcome found, unknown, failed, skipped or unchanged, and at
    the end a sentence of the counts on standard error. The exit status is
    0 when the run finished with no failure. --overwrite asks again for
    what is already held (ES-DE's "Overwrite files and data"); --region,
    --language and --threads are the run's choices, each bounded by the
    scraper's rules and the account's limits. --resume continues a run
    that was interrupted, with the games it had queued. A run already
    going, whether started in EmuSen or in another shell, is refused.

    A run that outlasts the line's budget goes on in the background as
    scrape<n>: 'ps' lists it with how many games are done, 'scrape wait'
    follows it again, and 'kill scrape<n>' cancels it (the games not
    reached are not asked).

    'status' prints the quota as the scraper holds it (quota.<field>:
    today's requests, the day's limit, threads, the pace, whether it is
    stopped and until when), whether a member account is signed in and
    when it was last verified, where the developer credentials come from
    (tree, user, embedded or none), and the run going. It never prints an
    account's name or a password, nor any credential; the credentials are
    the scraper's, kept as they always were.

FILES
    Media/media.db and Media/...    the scraper's store, the only place
                                    pictures are written
    <log folder>/scrape_<yyyyMMdd>.log
                                    one line a ScreenScraper request (its
                                    game, kind, status, bytes, time, worker
                                    and waits) and one a run, in the error
                                    log's folder; no credential is written
                                    (EmuSen_LibraryTools.md section 9.10)
    The library is only read.

EXAMPLES
    scrape ls --system snes --kinds theme
        each SNES game and where each kind its theme draws comes from
    scrape ls --kinds theme --missing any | scrape plan --kinds theme
        the gaps the theme would show, and what filling them would cost
    scrape plan --favorites --kinds cover,marquee,3dbox
    scrape run --collection Couch --videos
    scrape status | grep '^quota\.'

SEE ALSO
    rom(1), pkg(1), ps(1), kill(1); EmuSen_LibraryTools.md, section 8;
